WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:08.720
Ok, so, all gripes against Spider-Man 3 aside,
we still love Sam Raimi's trilogy for its

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.049
heartfelt take on the amazing wall-crawler.
The studio basically made him shoehorn Venom

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:17.760
into the third movie, overruling his wishes
to make Sandman its only villain and ruining

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.750
the chances for Spider-Man 4.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.600
But thanks to some concept art by storyboard
artist Jeffrey Henderson, we can see that

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.789
Spidey would've taken on the Vulture and Mysterio...if
Raimi had managed to hang around for a fourth

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:30.760
movie, that is.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.590
Busting Mysterio

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.329
The storyboard concept art for Mysterio doesn't
really show a fight between him and Spidey,

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:40.480
but we can imagine how great it might have
been. Mysterio mainly uses special effects,

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.530
illusions, hypnotism, and theatrics, so he
may not have been too serious of a threat

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.120
compared to the rest of Spider-Man's foes.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.340
That unmasked Mysterio at the police station
looks...kind of like Bruce Campbell, right?

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.820
Given Raimi's longtime friendship with Campbell,
it would've been the perfect casting. The

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.760
Evil Dead star appeared in all three Spider-Man
movies that Raimi directed.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.620
First he was the announcer who named Peter
Parker "Spider-Man."

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.760
Then he was the snooty theater usher in the
sequel.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.479
And he finally played the French restaurant
maître d' in Spider-Man 3.

00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:10.759
We can almost imagine Mysterio's backstory
just based on Campbell's three appearances

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:14.700
in the previous Spider-Man films. He wanted
to get into show business, but had to settle

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.490
as a lowly wrestling announcer. Then he tried
to work in theater, but never made it. As

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.479
a result, he became an usher. But when that
didn't work out, he ultimately resorted to

00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:24.380
a restaurant gig where he used his acting
skills to fake a French accent.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.990
In a bind for cash, he stole a bunch of special
effects equipment for a robbery, becoming

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:31.319
Mysterio—only to be busted by Spider-Man.

00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:35.420
How great would it have been to see Maguire
and Campbell facing off one last time?

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.979
The Vulture takes flight

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:41.109
These storyboard scenes of the Vulture seem
a lot more serious in tone than Mysterio's,

00:01:41.109 --> 00:01:43.609
suggesting that he may have been the movie's
main villain.

00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:46.779
You can see him reaching for a helicopter
and taking on Spider-Man in the skies above

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:47.299
New York.

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:50.369
And it looks like the Vulture's suit is very
much in sync with the character's original

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:54.219
comic book outfit. This is pretty different
from the first villain of the Spidey movies,

00:01:54.219 --> 00:01:58.130
Willem Dafoe's Green Goblin, who has a modernized
look compared to the source material.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:02.689
Well, what do you think? Would a fourth movie
have righted the wrongs of Spider-Man 3? Or

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:06.689
would the Vulture and Mysterio have been the
final fall for Tobey Maguire's Spider-Man?

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.930
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.560
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know who you think should've played Spider-Man

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.340
4's villains...

